      "hints": [
        "Substitute the proven identity: $\\frac{2}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta} = \\frac{6\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}$.",
        "Cancel $\\sin\\theta$ and solve $\\cos^2\\theta = \\frac{1}{3} \\implies \\cos\\theta = \\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.",
        "Find both angles in degrees for $0^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Substitute the identity into the given trigonometric equation:\n$$\\frac{2}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta} = \\frac{6}{\\tan\\theta} = \\frac{6\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}$$",
        "Multiply through by $\\sin\\theta$ (valid since $\\sin\\theta \\ne 0$ for $0^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$):\n$$\\frac{2}{\\cos\\theta} = 6\\cos\\theta \\implies 6\\cos^2\\theta = 2 \\implies \\cos^2\\theta = \\frac{1}{3}$$",
        "Take square roots to find both positive and negative values:\n$$\\cos\\theta = \\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\approx \\pm 0.57735$$",
        "Solve for $\\theta$ in the range $0^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$:\n$$\\text{From } \\cos\\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}: \\quad \\theta_1 = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\approx 54.7^\\circ$$\n$$\\text{From } \\cos\\theta = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}: \\quad \\theta_2 = 180^\\circ - 54.74^\\circ \\approx 125.3^\\circ$$\n$$(\\text{Solutions: } \\theta = 54.7^\\circ, \\quad 125.3^\\circ)$$"
      ],
